I committed Slice 1 and implemented Slice 2: `rb_foreach` is now a non-recursive walk over parent pointers, and all four gates pass. Slice 2 is not committed yet.

## Slice 1 commit
- `git diff --check` was clean and `tests/test_rbtree.c` was the only modified file.
- Committed as **`f2fe3a8`** `M3: foreach shape/order/restoration/read-only-callback tests`. The working tree was clean afterwards.

## Slice 2, red step (not committed)
I turned on `rb_foreach` for the 20,000-node left spine with the old recursive version still in place:
- `./build/test_rbtree --stress 20000` crashed with a segmentation fault (exit 139) on the heap left spine, right after the 64 KB worker thread started.
- `make asan` named the cause: `ERROR: AddressSanitizer: stack-overflow … (T2)`, with frames repeating `foreach_inorder src/rbtree.c:358`, the left-child recursion.

All three outputs are saved in the scratchpad under `scratchpad/red/` (`stress20000.out`, `make_test.out`, `make_asan.out`).

## Slice 2, implementation diff
`src/rbtree.c` (+38/−8): `foreach_inorder` is removed and `rb_foreach` is replaced:
```c
void rb_foreach(const rbtree_t *t,
                void (*fn)(const char *key, void *value, void *ctx), void *ctx) {
    const struct rb_node *cur = t->root;
    if (cur == NULL) return;
    while (cur->left != NULL) cur = cur->left;
    /* Invariant: exactly the keys < cur->key have been passed to fn, each once. */
    while (cur != NULL) {
        fn(cur->key, cur->value, ctx);
        if (cur->right != NULL) {
            /* Successor is the leftmost node of the right subtree. */
            cur = cur->right;
            while (cur->left != NULL) cur = cur->left;
        } else {
            /* Climb past every ancestor we reached from its right side; the
             * first one reached from the left is next, NULL means done. */
            const struct rb_node *child;
            do {
                child = cur;
                cur = cur->parent;
            } while (cur != NULL && child == cur->right);
        }
    }
}
```
The comment above it states the callback rules:
- **Allowed:** read-only calls on the same tree (`rb_find`, `rb_size`, `rb_validate`, a nested `rb_foreach`), and anything on other trees.
- **Forbidden:** `rb_insert`, `rb_delete` and `rb_destroy` on the tree being walked. No mutating call is allowed, not even on the node just visited.
- **Callback that never returns** (exit or longjmp): the tree stays valid, because the walk never writes to it and holds nothing.

`tests/test_rbtree.c` (+5/−7): the stress harness now runs `rb_foreach` on both shapes, and the "visited all N keys in order" check is no longer limited to the balanced tree. The `run_foreach` field is now always `true`; I left it in to keep the diff small. Removing it is a two-line cleanup if you'd rather.

No changes to `include/`, `src/pool.c`, the Makefile or `rb_destroy`. `git diff --check` is clean.

## Verification (each from a clean rebuild)
| Gate | Result |
|---|---|
| `make test` | exit 0. 5403 unit checks (heap 2390 / pooled 2366, **difference 24**) + 36 stress checks (was 34: one new left-spine order check per mode); fuzz at 10⁵ operations OK in both modes |
| `make asan` | exit 0. 5439 PASS, 0 FAIL, 0 ASan/UBSan/leak reports |
| `make stress-asan` | exit 0. 36 PASS, 0 FAIL. **heap and pooled `[stress left spine] foreach visited all 1000000 keys in order`** on the 64 KB thread; 6.0 s |
| `make memcheck` | exit 0. 4 runs, all "All heap blocks were freed" and 0 errors (allocation counts 7,806 / 20,455 / 15,636 / 120,484; the fuzz counts differ from last time only because the seed is random) |

**Fixed stack:** `-fstack-usage` reports `rb_foreach` as a fixed-size (`static`) frame: 32 B at -O1/-O2, 64 B at -O0, the same as `rb_destroy`. In the disassembly the only `call` inside it is `call *%r8`, the user callback. `foreach_inorder` no longer appears anywhere in src/, include/ or tests/.

**O(n) time:** I counted pointer moves in an instrumented scratch copy (not in the repo) on balanced (heap), left spine (pooled) and right spine (pooled) trees, n = 1 to 10⁶. My plan predicted 2(n−1) moves, but the measured count is **2n−1** for every shape and size. The extra move is the final step from the root up to NULL, which ends the loop. Each edge is still walked once down and once up, so the cost is linear. I'll write it as 2n−1 in the docs.

**Tree unchanged and no allocation:**
- The Slice 1 checks (rb_find results, size, parent links and rb_validate, compared before and after the walk, plus zero rb_malloc calls during it) all pass against the new code in both modes.
- The stress harness confirms zero allocations during foreach plus destroy at 2×10⁴ and 10⁶.

`git status`: ` M src/rbtree.c`, ` M tests/test_rbtree.c`.

If you approve, I'll commit as `M3: O(1)-space parent-pointer rb_foreach + left-spine stress`, then Slice 3 (docs). The hostile review and Mutation 4 aren't started.

